  "result": "I re-planned `NRS_484B_307_9c` and the validator now returns `accept`. It has not been coded or gated yet. `aggregates.jsonl` is empty because this law needs no law-local aggregate.\n\n**The anchor fix.** Iteration 6 failed the gate because the traffic signal head `h` was tied to the pedestrian only through position arithmetic. It is now tied by relations:\n- `h` controls an intersection `i`, either directly or through an approach lane that enters `i`.\n- The pedestrian's body must be within the facing reach of `i`. That reach already limits how far the faced head can be, so this condition adds no real restriction.\n\n**The exception, now two spans:**\n- **\"unless permitted to proceed by another device\"** is written directly with atoms. The permission holds when a pedestrian signal governs the roadway or lane being entered, the subject faces it, and it shows walk or flashing don't-walk with a countdown. These are the permissions in NRS 484B.283(1)(d)(1) and (2).\n- **The bracketed reference** composes the four 283 nodes as exceptions. I first tried treating it as a borrowed-words reference, but the validator rejected that. It requires the reference to be composed as rules.\n\n**One composition does nothing.** `NRS_484B_283_1d1` has a vehicle as its subject, so mapped to a pedestrian it never applies. I kept it only because the validator requires every named law to be composed. The walk permission it would have supplied is carried by the exception span above. I flagged this for you in `PLAN.md`.\n\n**Reading of \"enter the highway\".** I read it as stepping onto a roadway or lane from somewhere that is not a roadway, lane, median or safety zone. This matches how the accepted 283(1)(d)(3)/(4) nodes read \"entered the highway\". A pedestrian continuing across from a median does not count as entering.\n\n**Scenarios.** Beyond the four standard ones, there are four branches:\n- walk shown: not applicable\n- flashing don't-walk with countdown: not applicable\n- steady don't-walk: not applicable, because the 283(1)(d)(4) rule takes over and judges the step\n- stepping off a median: compliant\n\n`PLAN.md` also says what would change these decisions: if the gate still finds `h` unanchored, tie its governed region to the pedestrian directly.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/fsw/portfolios/av/projects/av_alpamayo_reasoning/users/wcong/WorldModelSpec/.worktrees/full221-v2/pipeline/traffic-spec-agent/results/full221-1116-1018/iter-007/plan/`:\n- `blueprint.jsonl`\n- `aggregates.jsonl`\n- `PLAN.md`",
